{"parentUuid":"775f8e0f-b589-442d-8a86-27319ce12e89","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0121RsFy1EWZhvsLuYHEVwAx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Claude 3.7 Sonnet extended thinking reasoning combinatorial benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Claude 3.7 Sonnet and Claude Code\",\"url\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-3-7-sonnet\"},{\"title\":\"Claude 3.7 Sonnet, extended thinking and long output\",\"url\":\"https://simonw.substack.com/p/claude-37-sonnet-extended-thinking\"},{\"title\":\"Better Math and Code? Testing Claude’s New 3.7 Sonnet Extended | XRAY Blog\",\"url\":\"https://www.xray.tech/post/claude-37-sonnet\"},{\"title\":\"Claude's extended thinking\",\"url\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/visible-extended-thinking\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluating Claude 3.7 Sonnet: Performance, reasoning ...\",\"url\":\"https://wandb.ai/byyoung3/Generative-AI/reports/Evaluating-Claude-3-7-Sonnet-Performance-reasoning-and-cost-optimization--VmlldzoxMTYzNDEzNQ\"},{\"title\":\"Claude 3.7 Sonnet Unveiled: Reviewing Anthropic’s Most Advanced Reasoning Model. | by Bernard Loki \\\"AI VISIONARY\\\" | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@bernardloki/claude-3-7-sonnet-unveiled-reviewing-anthropics-most-advanced-reasoning-model-772b74331226\"},{\"title\":\"Claude 3.7 Sonnet: How it Works, Use Cases & More | DataCamp\",\"url\":\"https://www.datacamp.com/blog/claude-3-7-sonnet\"},{\"title\":\"Claude 3.7 Sonnet Introduces Hybrid Reasoning and Extended Thinking\",\"url\":\"https://www.deeplearning.ai/the-batch/claude-3-7-sonnet-introduces-hybrid-reasoning-and-extended-thinking/\"},{\"title\":\"Anthropic’s Claude 3.7 Sonnet hybrid reasoning model is now available in Amazon Bedrock | Amazon Web Services\",\"url\":\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/anthropics-claude-3-7-sonnet-the-first-hybrid-reasoning-model-is-now-available-in-amazon-bedrock/\"},{\"title\":\"Beyond the Benchmark: A Hands-On Evaluation of Claude 3.7 Sonnet\",\"url\":\"https://hub.researchgraph.org/beyond-the-benchmark-a-hands-on-evaluation-of-claude-3-7-sonnet/\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive information about Claude 3.7 Sonnet's extended thinking capabilities and reasoning benchmarks. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Extended Thinking Reasoning\n\nClaude 3.7 Sonnet excels across instruction-following, general reasoning, multimodal capabilities, and agentic coding, with extended thinking providing a notable boost in math and science.\n\n## Combinatorial and Complex Reasoning Benchmarks\n\nClaude 3.7 Sonnet, tested with and without extended thinking (64K context), shows impressive results, particularly in graduate-level reasoning (78.2%/84.8%), multilingual Q&A (86.1%), instruction-following (93.2%), and math problem-solving (96.2%).\n\nClaude 3.7 achieves 78.2% accuracy in graduate-level reasoning tasks without extended thinking, and this improves to 84.8% with extended thinking enabled.\n\n## Software Engineering Benchmark\n\nClaude 3.7 Sonnet leads with 62.3% accuracy (70.3% with extended context) on SWE-bench software engineering benchmarks.\n\n## Math Competition Performance\n\nClaude 3.7 Sonnet makes a huge leap from previous versions, scoring 80.0% with extended thinking enabled on AIME 2024. While it beats DeepSeek-R1 (79.8%) by a small margin, it still lags behind OpenAI's o3-mini (87.3%) and Grok 3 Beta (93.3%).\n\n## How Extended Thinking Works\n\nWhen Claude 3.7 Sonnet is using its extended thinking capability, it benefits from \"serial test-time compute\"—it uses multiple, sequential reasoning steps before producing its final output, adding more computational resources as it goes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8cf5acb1-0b18-4df7-bb80-5c932975c49d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:07.413Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"775f8e0f-b589-442d-8a86-27319ce12e89","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"dcc8fd71-2888-4d6a-917b-05e502ce7f7c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016yXwvLWX7ASvskJYBNeBTt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Gemini 2.5 Pro thinking mode planning optimization benchmark 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Google I/O 2025: Updates to Gemini 2.5 from Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/google-deepmind/google-gemini-updates-io-2025/\"},{\"title\":\"Gemini 2.5: Our newest Gemini model with thinking\",\"url\":\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/google-deepmind/gemini-model-thinking-updates-march-2025/\"},{\"title\":\"Gemini 2.5 Pro Benchmarks & Pricing Developer Guide 2025\",\"url\":\"https://futureagi.com/blogs/gemini-2-5-pro-2025\"},{\"title\":\"‎Gemini Apps’ release updates & improvements\",\"url\":\"https://gemini.google/release-notes/\"},{\"title\":\"Think Fast, Think Smart: Optimizing Gemini 2.5 Pro with Thinking Budgets | by Angga Agia Wardhana | Google Cloud - Community | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/google-cloud/think-fast-think-smart-optimizing-gemini-2-5-pro-with-thinking-budgets-d1347bb49b5c\"},{\"title\":\"Gemini 2.5: Pushing the Frontier with Advanced Reasoning, Multimodality, Long\",\"url\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/gemini/gemini_v2_5_report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Gemini 2.5 Pro Review 2026: 1M Context, MCP & Deep Think | Future AGI Blog\",\"url\":\"https://futureagi.com/blog/google-gemini-2-5-pro-2025\"},{\"title\":\"Gemini 2.5 Pro - Intelligence, Performance & Price Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/gemini-2-5-pro\"},{\"title\":\"'Deep Think' boosts the performance of Google's flagship Google Gemini AI model | TechCrunch\",\"url\":\"https://techcrunch.com/2025/05/20/deep-think-boosts-the-performance-of-googles-flagship-google-gemini-ai-model/\"},{\"title\":\"Gemini 2.5: Pushing the Frontier with Advanced Reasoning, Multimodality, Long Context, and Next Generation Agentic Capabilities.\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.06261v1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Gemini 2.5 Pro's thinking mode and planning optimization in 2025:\n\n## Deep Think Enhanced Reasoning Mode\n\nGoogle announced Deep Think at Google I/O 2025, an enhanced reasoning mode for Gemini 2.5 Pro that allows the model to consider multiple answers to questions before responding, boosting its performance on certain benchmarks.\n\n## Benchmark Performance\n\nGemini 2.5 Pro Deep Think achieved an impressive score on the 2025 USAMO (one of the hardest math benchmarks), leads on LiveCodeBench (a difficult benchmark for competition-level coding), and scores 84.0% on MMMU, which tests multimodal reasoning.\n\nThe model demonstrated strong logical thinking and mathematical problem-solving skills, scoring 92.0% and 86.7% on AIME 2024 and 2025 benchmarks respectively.\n\n## Thinking Budget Optimization\n\nGoogle extended thinking budgets to 2.5 Pro for more control over the reasoning process. Thinking Budget is a setting in Gemini 2.5 that lets you control how much the model thinks, like choosing how much computational resources to allocate before it replies.\n\n## Model Architecture\n\nGemini 2.5 Pro is the most capable model yet, achieving state-of-the-art performance on frontier coding and reasoning benchmarks, and is a thinking model that excels at multimodal understanding.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7150d6e1-540f-41a0-ad00-4a78dce5f7a5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:08.864Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dcc8fd71-2888-4d6a-917b-05e502ce7f7c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b7f59966-09af-4546-8ce8-984dc8787f7f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSpZ719xkADT65keChzopo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LLM candidate ranking heuristic CP-SAT branch and bound prompt\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OPTIMIZABLE LLM PLANNING: A BRANCH-AND- BOUND ...\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=QxioUQeZO9\"},{\"title\":\"PPO guided Agentic Pipeline for Adaptive Prompt Selection and Test Case Generation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2605.00942\"},{\"title\":\"Learning Rate Based Branching Heuristic for SAT Solvers | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/303901008_Learning_Rate_Based_Branching_Heuristic_for_SAT_Solvers\"},{\"title\":\"Learning Rate Based Branching Heuristic for SAT Solvers | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-40970-2_9\"},{\"title\":\"Combining Clause Learning and Branch and Bound for MaxSAT\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2021.38\"},{\"title\":\"AutoV: Loss-Oriented Ranking for Visual Prompt Retrieval in LVLMs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2506.16112\"},{\"title\":\"LLM-Guided Heuristic Search\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/llm-guided-heuristic-search\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"SolSearch: An LLM-Driven Framework for Efficient SAT-Solving Code Generation * Equal contribution. This work was supported by National Key Research and Development Program (No.2022YFB3305200), NSFC (No.62161146001, 92370201), STCSM (No.22QB1402100). (Corresponding author: Xiangfeng Wang and Jianqi Shi)\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.14328v1\"},{\"title\":\"Prompting-based Methods for Text Ranking Using Large Language Models - Sumit's Diary\",\"url\":\"https://blog.reachsumit.com/posts/2023/12/prompting-llm-for-ranking/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about your query. Here's what I discovered:\n\n## Key Findings\n\nThe most directly relevant work is on Optimizable LLM Planning (OLP), which formulates planning as a plan ranking problem where candidate plans are ordered by their estimated utility, where a plan's utility captures its likelihood of being the optimal choice within the given budget.\n\n### OLP Framework and Branch-and-Bound\n\nThe OLP approach maintains a utility-driven branch-and-bound search that adapts continuously and efficiently as reasoning unfolds, using heuristic models to calibrate reward and cost in a principled way through approximating branch-and-bound by providing lower and upper utility estimates.\n\nEach plan is decomposed into a simple subtask that can be solved directly and a residual subtask that requires further reasoning, where each decomposition produces a distinct branch consisting of an immediately solvable subtask and a residual subtask.\n\n### Ranking and Bounding Process\n\nThe reward from the solvable subtask can be assessed directly through a task execution operator together with a reward model, while the residual subtask is evaluated indirectly by assigning lower and upper bounds on its utility.\n\nThe approach identifies a plan whose lower bound dominates all other upper bounds, allowing it to efficiently identify the best plan without fully executing every candidate.\n\n## Related Research\n\nThe search also revealed frameworks that employ LLMs for comparative heuristic feedback (ranking candidate solutions rather than assigning absolute values) demonstrate robust empirical gains in domains where constraint satisfaction or subjective criteria are difficult to model.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b39ec2d9-342e-4d4c-bbfe-a4af92dd7ae4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:09.630Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b7f59966-09af-4546-8ce8-984dc8787f7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6eb1a761-9290-49a7-811f-737d9a3a54d6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014LcYaVrn7G6RZmuCR2uQxQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"o1 reasoning model token cost pricing latency benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Comparison of AI Models across Intelligence, Performance, and Price\",\"url\":\"https://artificialanalysis.ai/models\"},{\"title\":\"Reasoning models | OpenAI API\",\"url\":\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning\"},{\"title\":\"o1 - Intelligence, Performance & Price Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/o1\"},{\"title\":\"o1-pro Model | OpenAI API\",\"url\":\"https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/o1-pro\"},{\"title\":\"Best Reasoning Model APIs | Compare Cost, Context & Scalability\",\"url\":\"https://www.clarifai.com/blog/best-reasoning-model-apis/\"},{\"title\":\"OpenAI o1-mini | OpenAI\",\"url\":\"https://openai.com/index/openai-o1-mini-advancing-cost-efficient-reasoning/\"},{\"title\":\"Pricing | OpenAI API\",\"url\":\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing\"},{\"title\":\"Estimating costs of O1 queries - API - OpenAI Developer Community\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/estimating-costs-of-o1-queries/943622\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Pricing: Top 15+ Providers Compared\",\"url\":\"https://aimultiple.com/llm-pricing\"},{\"title\":\"GPT‑4.1 nano Pricing Calculator | Fast, Free, No Sign-Up\",\"url\":\"https://livechatai.com/gpt-4-1-nano-pricing-calculator\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for information about the o1 reasoning model's token cost, pricing, latency, and benchmarks. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Performance & Benchmarking:**\no1 achieves a score of 31 on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index, a composite benchmark that evaluates models across reasoning, knowledge, mathematics, and coding. At 104 tokens per second, o1 is faster than average (76).\n\n**Pricing:**\nO3 (the newer model) is priced at $10/M input tokens and $40/M output tokens, with cached input tokens costing $2.50/M. While specific current pricing for o1 wasn't fully detailed in the results, o1-mini was launched at a cost that is 80% cheaper than OpenAI o1-preview.\n\n**Token Costs & Usage:**\nBenchmark research shows test-time compute costs for reasoning models can be 25× higher than standard chat models. For example, benchmarking OpenAI's O1 cost $2,767 because it produced 44 million tokens. Reasoning models can generate 10–30× more thinking tokens per request.\n\n**Latency:**\nChatGPT Plus, Team, Enterprise, and Edu users can use o1-mini as an alternative to o1-preview, with higher rate limits and lower latency. o1-mini reached answers around 3-5x faster compared to o1-preview on certain reasoning tasks.\n\n**Model Capabilities:**\nIt uses extended thinking or chain-of-thought reasoning to work through complex problems before providing an answer. o1 has a context window of 200k tokens.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cfc13d84-caaf-4ec7-97f3-4c94275c2282","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:10.069Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6eb1a761-9290-49a7-811f-737d9a3a54d6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
