{"parentUuid":"db6e9bdc-3613-4da7-b22e-c4aa77ae65b7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01X5NpbeqxRw14KaPbrrA5td","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Pn5jfeHFK83uVcr7KLvt5K","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"chain of thought\" branching variable ordering CP-SAT user propagator LLM"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7146,"cache_read_input_tokens":25653,"output_tokens":311,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7146},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":311,"cache_read_input_tokens":25653,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7146,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7146,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaosPW7Lqj6Zvyjp2HFXv","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b12a90cc-d68b-4127-aa3a-13761004b33d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:15.487Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"2016ee42-a72b-4acf-9caa-be78e91adeda","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RfoegNjZ8CqhvDMuKiYJVN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"AlphaProof IMO Lean formal reasoning combinatorial optimization application\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Olympiad-level formal mathematical reasoning with reinforcement learning | Nature\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09833-y\"},{\"title\":\"AI achieves silver-medal standard solving International Mathematical Olympiad problems — Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/blog/ai-solves-imo-problems-at-silver-medal-level/\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaProof Paper\",\"url\":\"https://www.julian.ac/blog/2025/11/13/alphaproof-paper/\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaProof and the IMO - by Greg Burnham - Lemmata\",\"url\":\"https://lemmata.substack.com/p/alphaproof-and-the-imo\"},{\"title\":\"AI math genius delivers 100% accurate results\",\"url\":\"https://phys.org/news/2025-11-ai-math-genius-accurate-results.html\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaProof: AI for Formal Mathematics\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/alphaproof\"},{\"title\":\"Google DeepMind's Latest Paper in Nature Unveils Most Powerful Mathematical Model for IMO\",\"url\":\"https://eu.36kr.com/en/p/3551304246589575\"},{\"title\":\"CombiBench: Benchmarking LLM Capability for Combinatorial Mathematics\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.03171\"},{\"title\":\"Olympiad-level formal mathematical reasoning with reinforcement learning - PubMed\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41225005/\"},{\"title\":\"Formal Mathematical Reasoning: A New Frontier in AI\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.16075\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's comprehensive information about AlphaProof and its application to IMO, Lean formal reasoning, and combinatorial optimization:\n\n## Overview\n\nAlphaProof is a system that trains itself to prove mathematical statements in the formal language Lean. It is a new reinforcement-learning based system for formal math reasoning, and together with AlphaGeometry 2, solved four out of six problems from this year's International Mathematical Olympiad (IMO), achieving the same level as a silver medalist in the competition for the first time.\n\n## How It Works\n\nWhen presented with a problem, AlphaProof generates solution candidates and then proves or disproves them by searching over possible proof steps in Lean. Each proof that was found and verified is used to reinforce AlphaProof's language model, enhancing its ability to solve subsequent, more challenging problems.\n\n## Performance on IMO\n\nAlphaProof solved three problems (P1, P2 and P6) while AlphaGeometry 2 solved one (P4), scoring 28 points total, which places the performance of the system within the silver-medal threshold.\n\n## Combinatorial Optimization Challenges\n\nA significant challenge for AlphaProof is combinatorics. Before the IMO 2024 competition, the final auto-formalization process achieved a 60% pass@1 success rate on the 50 representative IMO problems, with notable strengths in algebra (81.3%) and number theory (76.9%), and a lower rate for combinatorics (33.3%). The remaining two unsolved problems by AlphaProof were combinatorial.\n\n## Formal Verification Advantage\n\nAlphaProof's answers are always 100% correct because it uses a specialized software environment called Lean that acts like a strict teacher verifying every logical step.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d239e5f3-c01a-4f96-afd6-7e2a11a84002","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:22.379Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2016ee42-a72b-4acf-9caa-be78e91adeda","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b12a90cc-d68b-4127-aa3a-13761004b33d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pn5jfeHFK83uVcr7KLvt5K","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"chain of thought\" branching variable ordering CP-SAT user propagator LLM\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Demystifying Chains, Trees, and Graphs of Thoughts\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2401.14295v5\"},{\"title\":\"Conditional Logic and Branching in LLM Orchestration | llm-exe\",\"url\":\"https://llm-exe.com/examples/chains/conditional-logic-with-llms.html\"},{\"title\":\"Scaling Graph Chain-of-Thought Reasoning: A Multi-Agent Framework with Efficient LLM Serving\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.01633v1\"},{\"title\":\"[2501.18645] Layered Chain-of-Thought Prompting for Multi-Agent LLM Systems: A Comprehensive Approach to Explainable Large Language Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18645\"},{\"title\":\"Guiding Agent Reasoning with Chain-of-Thought\",\"url\":\"https://apxml.com/courses/intro-llm-agents/chapter-5-basic-agent-planning/guiding-agent-reasoning-chain-of-thought\"},{\"title\":\"Prompt Structure Chaining for LLMs: Ultimate Guide\",\"url\":\"https://orq.ai/blog/prompt-structure-chaining\"},{\"title\":\"Layered Chain-of-Thought Prompting for Multi-Agent LLM Systems: A Comprehensive Approach to Explainable Large Language Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.18645v2\"},{\"title\":\"Have Large Language Models Learned to Reason? A ...\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf/9c2fc7475e20f41686932bf67d787220ec571d91.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Chain-of-Thought Prompting\",\"url\":\"https://learnprompting.org/docs/intermediate/chain_of_thought\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about \"chain of thought\" branching variable ordering CP-SAT user propagator LLM. Here are the relevant results:\n\nThe search found information across several domains:\n\n**Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and LLMs:**\nTree of Thoughts (ToT) elevates the CoT limitations by allowing prompt branching at any point of the chain of thoughts. Recent research addresses Graph-CoT designs and co-designs reasoning frameworks with LLM serving architecture, presenting systems like GLM, a multi-agent Graph-CoT LLM framework with optimized serving that improves accuracy, latency, and token efficiency.\n\n**CP-SAT and Constraint Programming:**\nThe search also returned information about CP-SAT, which is Google OR-Tools' constraint programming solver. Branching in CP-SAT can utilize LP optimal value and reduced costs. The propagation in CP-SAT is EXACT.\n\nHowever, the search results don't appear to contain substantial information directly connecting all these concepts together (chain of thought reasoning, branching strategies, variable ordering in CP-SAT, user propagators, and LLMs simultaneously). The results either focus on LLM reasoning techniques separately or on CP-SAT constraint solving separately. If you're looking for research that specifically integrates these areas, you may need to search for more specialized academic papers or implementation documentation.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9efb1269-75c8-4bc5-b0dd-c076a1343867","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:22.392Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b12a90cc-d68b-4127-aa3a-13761004b33d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"dec70a07-22a7-4a98-86de-98f4a5e95779","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NcsgXzYhddivxFyTRpjrBU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"FunSearch DeepMind code generation combinatorial vs LLM as optimizer comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"FunSearch: Making new discoveries in mathematical sciences using Large Language Models — Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/blog/funsearch-making-new-discoveries-in-mathematical-sciences-using-large-language-models/\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch.pdf - Computer Science\",\"url\":\"https://cs.nyu.edu/~davise/papers/FunSearch.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mathematical discoveries from program search with large language models | Nature\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06924-6\"},{\"title\":\"This is the author’s version of the work accepted in Nature.\",\"url\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/DeepMind.com/Blog/funsearch-making-new-discoveries-in-mathematical-sciences-using-large-language-models/Mathematical-discoveries-from-program-search-with-large-language-models.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch — How Deepmind uses LLM to solve a Mathematic problem that has never been solved | by Fisher Lok | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://fisherlok.medium.com/funsearch-how-deepmind-uses-llm-to-solve-a-mathematic-problem-that-has-never-been-solved-17a9f705f771\"},{\"title\":\"Inside FunSearch: Google DeepMind’s New LLM that is Able to Discover New Math and Computer Science Algorithms | by Jesus Rodriguez | Towards AI\",\"url\":\"https://pub.towardsai.net/inside-funsearch-google-deepminds-new-llm-that-is-able-to-discover-new-math-and-computer-science-2484b7dd89ad?gi=a2887933f233\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch: Making new discoveries in mathematical sciences using Large Language Models - Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/funsearch-making-new-discoveries-in-mathematical-sciences-using-large-language-models/\"},{\"title\":\"Can We Use AI to Discover Better Algorithms? | Richard Cornelius Suwandi\",\"url\":\"https://richardcsuwandi.github.io/blog/2025/llm-algorithm-discovery/\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch Algorithm: LLM-Guided Evolutionary Search\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/funsearch-algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - google-deepmind/funsearch · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/funsearch\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of FunSearch and how it compares LLM-driven approaches with combinatorial optimization:\n\n## FunSearch Overview\n\nFunSearch is a method for searching for \"functions\" written in computer code that works by pairing a pre-trained LLM, whose goal is to provide creative solutions in the form of computer code, with an automated \"evaluator\", which guards against hallucinations and incorrect ideas.\n\n## Key Distinction: LLM as Optimizer vs. Direct Solver\n\nA crucial insight from FunSearch's design is how it uses the LLM differently from traditional approaches:\n\nFunSearch uses the LLM as a subroutine in a genetic programming algorithm, to generate mutations of one particular subroutine in a larger program. The LLM is not told either what problem is being addressed or what the overall program is. This contrasts with simply asking an LLM to solve a problem directly.\n\n## Combinatorial Problem Applications\n\nFunSearch showcases the possibility of solving notoriously hard combinatorial problems (problems that are easy to evaluate but hard to solve) when combining LLM and evolutionary algorithms. \n\nSpecific applications include:\n- The cap set problem in extremal combinatorics—discovering new constructions of large cap sets going beyond the best-known ones\n- Online bin packing, a problem in combinatorial optimization\n\n## Performance Against Traditional Approaches\n\nFunSearch outperformed state-of-the-art computational solvers, as this problem scales well beyond their current capabilities. This demonstrates an advantage over purely computational approaches for large-scale combinatorial problems.\n\n## Technical Strategy\n\nFunSearch samples best performing programs and feeds them back into prompts for the LLM to improve on (best-shot prompting), starts with a program skeleton containing boilerplate code and only evolves the critical program logic (for example, by setting a greedy program skeleton and evolving a priority function), maintains a large pool of diverse programs by using an island-based evolutionary method that encourages exploration and avoids local optima.\n\nThis approach effectively combines the creative code generation capabilities of LLMs with the systematic optimization power of evolutionary algorithms to tackle hard combinatorial problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d329f7ad-a5c4-40ad-b7bc-04503ecf8ad1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:23.401Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dec70a07-22a7-4a98-86de-98f4a5e95779","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"db6e9bdc-3613-4da7-b22e-c4aa77ae65b7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KstnUL5iRmkqfwdJiKRn7F","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LLM tool use OR-Tools agent code interpreter optimization solver 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Build Your Own Code Interpreter - Dynamic Tool Generation and Execution With o3-mini\",\"url\":\"https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/examples/object_oriented_agentic_approach/secure_code_interpreter_tool_for_llm_agents\"},{\"title\":\"Agentic Code Optimization via Compiler-LLM Cooperation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.04238v1\"},{\"title\":\"A Survey on Code Generation with LLM-based Agents\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2508.00083v1\"},{\"title\":\"Custom Optimization Tools for LLM: Best Practices and Comparison\",\"url\":\"https://www.patronus.ai/llm-testing/custom-optimization-tools-for-llm\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - AGI-Edgerunners/LLM-Agents-Papers: A repo lists papers related to LLM based agent · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/AGI-Edgerunners/LLM-Agents-Papers\"},{\"title\":\"[2604.04238] Agentic Code Optimization via Compiler-LLM Cooperation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.04238\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Agents | Prompt Engineering Guide<!-- -->\",\"url\":\"https://www.promptingguide.ai/research/llm-agents\"},{\"title\":\"Compiled AI: Deterministic Code Generation for LLM-Based Workflow Automation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2604.05150\"},{\"title\":\"Agentic Reasoning and Tool Integration for LLMs via Reinforcement Learning\",\"url\":\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/AgenticReasoning.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Agent-as-Tool: A Study on the Hierarchical Decision Making with Reinforcement Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.01489v1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about LLM tool use, OR-Tools agents, code interpreters, and optimization solvers in 2025:\n\n## Recent Developments in LLM Tool Use and Agent Optimization (2025)\n\n### Code Interpretation and Dynamic Tool Generation\n\nDevelopers are exploring more flexible paradigms to dynamically generate tools using LLM models (such as o3-mini), with the ability to execute tools using a code interpreter. The Dynamic Tool Calling approach allows LLMs to generate code and use a Code Interpreter tool to execute the code, overcoming limitations of predefined tool approaches.\n\n### Agentic Reasoning and Optimization\n\nResearchers propose compiler-LLM cooperation to balance the correctness of conventional compiler optimizations with the creativity of LLM-based code generation, integrating existing compiler optimization passes with LLM-based code generation at multiple levels of abstraction. This approach produces speedups up to 1.25x, outperforming both existing compiler optimizations and level-specific LLM-based baselines.\n\n### Tool Integration Frameworks\n\nARTIST (Agentic Reasoning and Tool Integration in Self-Improving Transformers) empowers LLMs to learn optimal strategies for leveraging external tools and interacting with complex environments via reinforcement learning. Recent advancements have explored integrating reinforcement learning into agent frameworks to improve decision-making over tool usage and multi-hop reasoning steps.\n\n### Compiled AI Approach\n\nA new paradigm involves \"Compilation over Interpretation\" where generated code is validated, tested, and deployed as static artifacts—the LLM exits the execution loop entirely.\n\nThe research indicates a shift toward more sophisticated multi-agent systems that combine the strengths of LLMs with traditional optimization tools and deterministic solvers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"210fa5c6-a294-4ba3-89a6-2397be736fb9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:24.167Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"db6e9bdc-3613-4da7-b22e-c4aa77ae65b7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
