{"parentUuid":"67d94e57-8120-4e3b-af25-2cd6dd455478","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LpzjArBuEP4dVphRJc2HKA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"o3 o4-mini API pricing per million tokens 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"o3 Mini API Pricing 2026 - Costs, Performance & Providers\",\"url\":\"https://pricepertoken.com/pricing-page/model/openai-o3-mini\"},{\"title\":\"o4 Mini API Pricing 2026 - Costs, Performance & Providers\",\"url\":\"https://pricepertoken.com/pricing-page/model/openai-o4-mini\"},{\"title\":\"GPT 4o mini API Pricing 2026 - Costs, Performance & Providers\",\"url\":\"https://pricepertoken.com/pricing-page/model/openai-gpt-4o-mini\"},{\"title\":\"o4-mini API Pricing 2026 - $1.1/$4.4 per 1M tokens\",\"url\":\"https://www.burnwise.io/ai-pricing/model/o4-mini\"},{\"title\":\"OpenAI API Pricing 2026: GPT-5.4, o3, o4-mini & All Models | AI Pricing Guru\",\"url\":\"https://www.aipricing.guru/openai-pricing/\"},{\"title\":\"Pricing | OpenAI API\",\"url\":\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing\"},{\"title\":\"Model prices | GPT for Work Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://gptforwork.com/help/resources/pricing-and-billing/model-prices\"},{\"title\":\"o4 Mini API Pricing (Updated April 2026) — Cost Calculator | TokenCost\",\"url\":\"https://tokencost.app/models/o4-mini\"},{\"title\":\"OpenAI o3 Pricing 2026: $2/$8 — But Hidden Tokens 3-10x Your Bill - TokenMix Blog\",\"url\":\"https://tokenmix.ai/blog/openai-o3-pricing\"},{\"title\":\"OpenAI API Pricing (Updated 2026) – All Models & Token Costs\",\"url\":\"https://pricepertoken.com/pricing-page/provider/openai\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here is the API pricing information for o3 and o4-mini as of 2026:\n\n**o3 Pricing:**\nThe o3 reasoning model costs $2.00 per million input tokens and $8.00 per million output tokens.\n\n**o4-mini Pricing:**\no4-mini pricing starts at $1.10 per million input tokens and $4.40 per million output tokens.\n\n**Important Note:**\nIt's worth noting that these reasoning models have additional complexity in their billing. Hidden reasoning tokens can inflate your actual bill 3-10x beyond what the price table suggests, as all internal reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens at $8.00/M (o3) or $4.40/M (o3-mini).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4011f213-2e96-4258-81d6-336e1d237b3a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:30.863Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"67d94e57-8120-4e3b-af25-2cd6dd455478","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"103c3ebc-776b-43f7-91b3-7bf222161e72","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019aUK4h4tcUrZUTD6kGWR3S","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"NP-Engine\" verifiable synthetic NP problems LLM benchmark 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"NP-Engine: Empowering Optimization Reasoning in Large Language Models with Verifiable Synthetic NP Problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.16476\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Benchmarks 2026 - Compare AI Benchmarks and Tests\",\"url\":\"https://llm-stats.com/benchmarks\"},{\"title\":\"[2510.26130] Beyond Synthetic Benchmarks: Evaluating LLM Performance on Real-World Class-Level Code Generation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26130\"},{\"title\":\"Beyond Synthetic Benchmarks: Evaluating LLM Performance on Real-World Class-Level Code Generation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.26130v1\"},{\"title\":\"The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions\",\"url\":\"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025\"},{\"title\":\"OPT-Engine: Benchmarking the Limits of LLMs in Optimization Modeling via Complexity Scaling\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.19924v1\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.24210\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Leaderboard 2026: Compare 300+ Top AI Models by Intelligence, Speed & Price\",\"url\":\"https://llm-stats.com/\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Research Papers: The 2025 List (July to December)\",\"url\":\"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-research-papers-2025-part2\"},{\"title\":\"AIME 2025 Benchmark Leaderboard\",\"url\":\"https://llm-stats.com/benchmarks/aime-2025\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found detailed information about NP-Engine:\n\nNP-Engine is a benchmark that focuses on utilizing LLMs to solve NP-hard problems, which involve complex combinatorial constraints and large problem spaces, posing significant optimization challenges. Since obtaining the optimal solution is computationally intractable in polynomial time, achieving better solutions requires the model to engage in advanced reasoning, iterative trial-and-error, the generation of initial feasible solutions, and continuous self-reflection and optimization.\n\nThe performance evaluation uses a range of benchmark datasets spanning multiple domains, including NP-Bench and five out-of-domain benchmarks categorized as: four reasoning benchmarks (Logic Reasoning: KOR-Bench; Math Reasoning: MATH500 and OlympiadBench; Knowledge Reasoning: GPQA_Diamond) and one Non-Reasoning Task: IFEVAL.\n\nA model called Qwen2.5-7B-NP was trained via zero-RLVR with curriculum learning on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, which significantly outperforms GPT-4o on NP-Bench and achieves SOTA performance with the same model size. Additionally, RLVR training on NP-Engine-Data enables strong out-of-domain (OOD) generalization to reasoning tasks (logic, puzzles, math, and knowledge), as well as non-reasoning tasks such as instruction following.\n\nThe research paper was published in October 2025 and is available on arXiv.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e4f01863-b832-427c-88de-5294e5853ce6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:34.653Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"103c3ebc-776b-43f7-91b3-7bf222161e72","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fc34fd48-8803-4de0-bec5-69fd35173287","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013qzyffgAodjPjLfe7wCrgd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"in-context learning few-shot TSP knapsack CVRP LLM accuracy 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Large Language Models as Hyper-Heuristics for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.01145v2\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Generalization of Neural Combinatorial Optimization for Vehicle Routing Problems via Test-Time Projection Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2506.02392\"},{\"title\":\"Select and Optimize: Learning to solve large-scale TSP instances Hanni Cheng\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v206/cheng23a/cheng23a.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Advancements in Zero-Shot and Few-Shot Learning for Large Language Models\",\"url\":\"https://www.goml.io/blog/advancements-in-zero-shot-and-few-shot-learning-for-large-language-models\"},{\"title\":\"1 Introduction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01068v1\"},{\"title\":\"Exploring Few-Shot Learning: Fine-Tuning vs. In-Context Learning and Parameter-Efficient Adaptations | Practice and Experience in Advanced Research Computing 2025: The Power of Collaboration\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3708035.3736091\"},{\"title\":\"Few-Shot Learning with LLMs: In-Context Learning | Keymakr\",\"url\":\"https://keymakr.com/blog/few-shot-learning-with-llms-in-context-learning-explained/amp/\"},{\"title\":\"[2005.14165] Language Models are Few-Shot Learners\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165\"},{\"title\":\"Many-Shot In-Context Learning - NIPS\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/file/8cb564df771e9eacbfe9d72bd46a24a9-Paper-Conference.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding in-context learning, few-shot learning, and LLM accuracy for combinatorial optimization problems in 2024:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**LLMs for Combinatorial Optimization Problems:**\n\nResearch in 2024 applied LLMs to diverse combinatorial optimization problems (COPs) including Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP), Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (CVRP), Multiple Knapsack Problem (MKP), and others.\n\n**ReEvo - Language Hyper-Heuristics Approach:**\n\nA 2024 paper introduced Reflective Evolution (ReEvo), which couples evolutionary computation with LLM reflections to achieve state-of-the-art sample efficiency for Language Hyper-Heuristics. ReEvo outperformed DeepACO (a neural heuristic baseline) on 3 out of 5 COPs, even when the neural model was overfitted to specific problem sizes (TSP50, OP50, and MKP100).\n\n**Few-Shot and In-Context Learning Performance:**\n\nFew-shot learning generally yields higher accuracy due to contextual guidance provided in the prompts. Many-shot in-context learning (ICL) consistently outperforms few-shot ICL, particularly on difficult non-natural language tasks.\n\n**Improvements on Neural Solvers:**\n\nLLM-based heuristic evolution improved the optimality gap of neural combinatorial optimization solvers, with POMO improving from 52% to 29% optimality gap on TSP1000.\n\nThe search results indicate significant progress in using LLMs with in-context learning for combinatorial optimization problems in 2024, showing competitive or superior performance compared to traditional neural and heuristic approaches.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fdd01bc4-4ba8-478d-81ba-41bd6c96d86a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:35.376Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fc34fd48-8803-4de0-bec5-69fd35173287","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a24c9cbe-f2f0-4348-b578-30c73b5ad63a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a55aa02cbc6f623ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ARihNZq9eJWMCYX9CEWCPf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LLM hint constraint solver branching priority small problem instance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"From Natural Language to Solver-Ready Power System Optimization: An LLM-Assisted, Validation-in-the-Loop Framework\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2508.08147\"},{\"title\":\"Hybrid Language Processor Fuzzing via LLM-Based ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity25-yang-yupeng.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - eth-sri/lmql: A language for constraint-guided and efficient LLM programming. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - metareflection/clpllm: CLP(LLM) -- using an LLM as a constraint solver\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/metareflection/clpllm\"},{\"title\":\"Combining Constraint Programming Reasoning with Large Language Model Predictions\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.13490v1\"},{\"title\":\"Large-Scale Constraint Generation Can LLMs Parse Hundreds of Constraints?\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.24090v1\"},{\"title\":\"ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial ...\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.809.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Hard Constraints Meet Soft Generation: Guaranteed Feasibility for LLM-based Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/400369786_Hard_Constraints_Meet_Soft_Generation_Guaranteed_Feasibility_for_LLM-based_Combinatorial_Optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Constraints | LMQL\",\"url\":\"https://lmql.ai/docs/language/constraints.html\"},{\"title\":\"Re-evaluating LLM-based Heuristic Search: A Case Study on the 3D Packing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.02297v1\"}]\n\nHere are the search results for your query about LLM hint constraint solver branching priority small problem instance:\n\nThe search returned several relevant papers and resources related to combining Large Language Models with constraint solving and branching strategies:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**1. LLM-Guided Branching Priorities for Solvers**\n\nResearch on power system optimization uses a message-passing GNN to score binary variables in MILP, with scores then mapped to integer priorities (e.g., Gurobi BranchPriority) to bias the search toward salient structure while preserving completeness and optimality. This approach empirically reduces explored nodes and wall-clock time on benchmarks.\n\n**2. LLM Context and Constraint Solving**\n\nPretrained LLMs are capable of picking up hints in the context that immediately enable them to solve constraints, without thoroughly analyzing the constraints in detail. This capability is particularly relevant for constraint satisfaction with limited context.\n\n**3. LLM-Enhanced Constraint Programs**\n\nLMQL supports advanced branching decoders like beam search and best_k for decoding algorithms. Additionally, in branching decoding, constraint validation will prune the current branch and continue generation only in the remaining, valid branches.\n\n**4. Learning-Based Branching Strategies**\n\nOne approach orders the domain of the current variable such that before the 10th word the solver tries the longer words first and at the 10th word the solver tries the shorter words first to guide search prioritization in small problem instances.\n\n**5. LLM-Based Constraint Solver Framework**\n\nConstraint programming problems are typically characterized by intricate combinatorial structures and a rich variety of constraints, especially powerful global constraints.\n\nThese resources show active research on integrating LLMs with constraint solvers, particularly for guiding branching decisions and prioritizing constraints in search.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"90619378-2963-4198-bd88-60281ba2a7e5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:37.595Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a24c9cbe-f2f0-4348-b578-30c73b5ad63a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
